        "1. Objective & Abstract\n",
        "The primary goal of this experiment is to systematically track and diagnose the mathematical phenomenon of reward over-optimization—commonly known as Goodhart's Law—within Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) pipelines. When proxy reward models are utilized to align a policy, excessive optimization pressure frequently causes the policy to exploit structural flaws or blind spots in the proxy model. This investigation explicitly measures where the over-optimization peak shifts by intentionally ablating two core operational variables: the physical model capacity (Small vs. Large architectures) and the total volume of preference training data (Scant vs. Abundant).\n",
        "2. Core Theoretical Framework\n",
        "Goodhart's Law states that when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. In machine learning alignment, this manifests as an over-optimization curve where the true objective and proxy objective decouple:\n",
        "**True Reward (R_true): **Evaluates true latent semantic value but enforces a strict mathematical penalty against superficial features like excessive length (verbosity bloating) or repetitive token combinations.\n",
        "**Proxy Reward Model (RM_theta): **Trained using a Bradley-Terry preference model via binary cross-entropy loss over noisy pairwise comparisons. These models act as approximations of human intent.\n",
        "**The Policy Agent (P_phi): **Optimizes its parameters directly against the proxy model using a simulated gradient ascent framework, driving up proxy evaluation metrics while inadvertently entering catastrophic blind spots.\n",
        "3. Experimental Configurations\n",
        "To track the peak shifts, three distinct operational testbeds were formulated:\n",
        "**1. Small RM / Scant Data: **Low-capacity linear model optimized with highly restricted data volume (40 noisy samples). Represents a severe high-bias, high-variance alignment constraint.\n",
        "**2. Small RM / Abundant Data: **Low-capacity linear model backed by substantial training volume (800 clean samples). Isolates structural model capacity constraints from data starvation.\n",
        "**3. Large RM / Abundant Data: **Deep neural network model utilizing multiple dense layers with non-linear activation functions, trained on 800 samples. Highly capable of mapping complex environments, but prone to high-variance over-fitting near noise boundaries.\n",
        "4. Analytical Summary Table\n",
        "Configuration Profile\tPeak Step Index\tMax True Reward\tPost-Peak Degradation Drop\n",
        "Small RM / Scant Data\tStep 5 to 12\tLow to Moderate Peak\tCatastrophic / Severe\n",
        "Small RM / Abundant Data\tDelayed (Step 20+)\tHigh & Stable Peak\tGradual / Controlled\n",
        "Large RM / Abundant Data\tSudden / Rapid Inflection\tVery High Peak\tRapid & Steep Crash\n",
        "5. Policy Exploitation Diagnosis\n",
        "The primary shortcut exploited by the policy across configurations is a superficial verbosity hack (modeled mathematically via high-magnitude feature accumulation). As optimization steps increase, the proxy model rewards feature patterns that resemble high quality but are structurally counterproductive. The Large RM discovers highly complex multi-dimensional boundaries; while this allows it to initially achieve a superior alignment peak, it introduces narrow adversarial valleys. Once the policy enters these valleys under extreme gradient pressure, true utility plummets catastrophically while the proxy score continuously accelerates upward.\n"
